                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "After division by ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "5^x",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", the left-hand side ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "(\\frac{2}{5})^x+(\\frac{3}{5})^x",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " is strictly decreasing and equals 1 at ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x=1",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Since $5^x>0$, divide both sides by $5^x$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\left(\\frac25\\right)^x+\\left(\\frac35\\right)^x&=1.\\end{align*}$$\nDefine\n$$f(x)=\\left(\\frac25\\right)^x+\\left(\\frac35\\right)^x.$$\nBoth bases lie between 0 and 1, so each term is strictly decreasing for real $x$. Their sum $f$ is therefore strictly decreasing.\n$$\\begin{align*}f(1)&=\\frac25+\\frac35\\\\f(1)&=1.\\end{align*}$$\nA strictly decreasing function takes the value 1 at most once. Thus $x=1$ is the only real solution.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
